\chapter{The FeedbackFruits API}
\label{API}

With an access token from \cref{Launch} we can use the API that FBF's own web
app uses.
This chapter is a thin client for it.
It knows how FBF speaks---JSON:API over HTTPS with a bearer token---but
nothing about activities, reviews or grades; that knowledge sits in the
modules that use it.
Keeping the client free of domain knowledge makes it the one place where we
talk to the network, which is also the place the tests record and replay.

\section{What the API looks like}

We learned the API from a browser capture and a few probes.
\begin{description}
\item[Format.] Responses follow JSON:API: a top-level [[data]] holding one
  resource or a list, an optional [[included]] list of related resources,
  and an optional [[meta]].
  A resource has a [[type]] (\eg [[progresses]], [[multimedia-tasks]]), an
  [[id]] (a UUID), [[attributes]] with kebab-case names, and
  [[relationships]].
  A relationship holds either [[data]] (the type and id of the related
  resource) or [[links]] with a [[related]] path to fetch it from, or both.
\item[Authentication.] The web app sends the token as a cookie together
  with a CSRF token, but the API accepts [[Authorization: Bearer]] with the
  same token, and nothing else.
  We use the header, which makes the client independent of cookies.
\item[Pagination.] A list request without [[page[number]]] returns the whole
  list and no [[meta]].
  The web app asks for pages, and then [[meta]] holds [[page]],
  [[per-page]], [[total-pages]] and [[total-count]]; pages count from~1, and
  page~0 is the same as page~1.
\item[Scope.] The token belongs to the user, not to the launched activity:
  [[/v1/courses]] lists every FBF course the user is in, and
  [[/v1/activities]] every activity.
\item[Renewal.] An access token lives 30~minutes.
  [[POST /oauth/token]] on the API host with [[grant_type=refresh_token]]
  gives a new access token and a new refresh token.
\end{description}

