\section{Paths and URLs}

We address the API with paths such as [[v1/activities/<id>]], and the API
itself gives us paths in relationship links, such as
[[/v1/activities/<id>/comments]].
Joining both kinds onto the API URL with [[urljoin]] treats them alike: a
leading slash means \enquote{from the host's root}, which is what FBF's links
mean.
A full URL passes through unchanged, so a link to another host still works.

<<imports>>=
import urllib.parse
<<functions>>=
def api_url_for(api_url, path):
    """Return the URL of `path` (relative, absolute or a full URL)."""
    return urllib.parse.urljoin(api_url.rstrip("/") + "/", path)
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_api_url_for_joins_paths_and_links():
  api = "https://api.example.com"
  assert api_url_for(api, "v1/users/current") \
    == "https://api.example.com/v1/users/current"
  assert api_url_for(api, "/v1/tasks/1/progresses") \
    == "https://api.example.com/v1/tasks/1/progresses"
  assert api_url_for(api, "https://other.example.com/x") \
    == "https://other.example.com/x"
@


\section{Resources and documents}

A JSON:API document is just nested dictionaries, and we could hand those to
the callers.
But every caller needs the same two things: to treat [[data]] uniformly
whether it is one resource or a list, and to find a related resource in
[[included]] by its type and id.
A small [[Document]] class does both once.
We keep the resources themselves as plain dictionaries, since the callers
read them through the schema checks of the extractors, not through methods
here.

The [[included]] index is keyed by [[(type, id)]] because ids are unique
only within a type in JSON:API.

<<classes>>=
class Document:
    """A JSON:API response document."""

    def __init__(self, document):
        self.raw = document
        data = document.get("data")
        self.is_list = isinstance(data, list)
        self.resources = data if self.is_list else [data] if data else []
        self.included = index_resources(document.get("included", []))
        self.meta = document.get("meta") or {}

    @property
    def resource(self):
        """The single resource of the document, `None` if there is none."""
        return self.resources[0] if self.resources else None
<<functions>>=
def index_resources(resources):
    """Return a dictionary of `resources` keyed by `(type, id)`."""
    return {(r["type"], r["id"]): r for r in resources}
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_document_treats_single_and_list_alike():
  one = Document({"data": {"type": "users", "id": "1"}})
  many = Document({"data": [{"type": "users", "id": "1"},
                            {"type": "users", "id": "2"}]})
  empty = Document({"data": []})
  assert not one.is_list and one.resource["id"] == "1"
  assert many.is_list and [r["id"] for r in many.resources] == ["1", "2"]
  assert empty.resources == [] and empty.resource is None

def test_document_indexes_included_by_type_and_id():
  doc = Document({"data": [],
                  "included": [{"type": "users", "id": "1"},
                               {"type": "emails", "id": "1"}]})
  assert doc.included[("users", "1")]["type"] == "users"
  assert doc.included[("emails", "1")]["type"] == "emails"
@

\subsection{Merging pages}

Since a list request without a page number returns the whole list, we
normally get one page.
We still merge pages when the response says there are more, in case FBF
starts paginating by default; otherwise we would silently get only the first
ten items.
Merging concatenates the data and unites the included resources.

<<functions>>=
def merge_pages(documents):
    """Merge JSON:API list documents (pages) into one document."""
    data, included = [], {}
    for document in documents:
        data.extend(document.get("data") or [])
        included.update(index_resources(document.get("included", [])))
    return {"data": data, "included": list(included.values())}
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_merge_pages_concatenates_and_dedups_included():
  user = {"type": "users", "id": "u"}
  pages = [{"data": [{"type": "progresses", "id": "1"}], "included": [user]},
           {"data": [{"type": "progresses", "id": "2"}], "included": [user]}]
  merged = Document(merge_pages(pages))
  assert [r["id"] for r in merged.resources] == ["1", "2"]
  assert list(merged.included) == [("users", "u")]
@

Whether more pages remain is a question for the [[meta]] of the last page.
Page~0 counts as page~1, hence the [[max]].

<<functions>>=
def next_page(meta):
    """Return the next page number after a page with `meta`, or `None`."""
    if not meta or "total-pages" not in meta:
        return None
    page = max(int(meta.get("page") or 1), 1)
    return page + 1 if page < int(meta["total-pages"]) else None
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_next_page():
  assert next_page({}) is None
  assert next_page({"page": 0, "total-pages": 3}) == 2
  assert next_page({"page": 2, "total-pages": 3}) == 3
  assert next_page({"page": 3, "total-pages": 3}) is None
@


\section{The client}

The client holds the tokens and a [[requests]] session.
Besides the tokens it can take a [[relaunch]] function, which returns a new
[[FBFLogin]], for when renewal fails; usually that is a [[launch]] with the
arguments of the first one.

<<imports>>=
import requests
<<classes>>=
class FBF:
    """A client for the FeedbackFruits API.

    `login` is an `FBFLogin` from `fbf.launch.launch`.  `relaunch`, if given,
    is a function returning a new `FBFLogin`, used when the tokens expire and
    cannot be refreshed.
    """

    def __init__(self, login, relaunch=None, session=None):
        self.login = login
        self.relaunch = relaunch
        self.session = session or requests.Session()
        self.renewal = threading.Lock()

    <<FBF methods>>
@

\subsection{Errors}

A failed request raises [[APIError]] with the status and the path, never
the response body: bodies may name students.

<<classes>>=
class APIError(Exception):
    """An FBF API request failed."""

    def __init__(self, status, method, url):
        self.status = status
        path = urllib.parse.urlsplit(url).path
        reason = "timed out" if status == "timeout" else f"HTTP {status}"
        super().__init__(f"{method} {path}: {reason}")
@

\subsection{Requests and renewal}

Every request goes through [[request]].
On a [[401]] it renews the tokens once and repeats the request; a second
[[401]] is an error, since then the new token is refused too and repeating
would loop.
That way callers never see the 30-minute expiry.

Requests may run in parallel (\cref{Parallel}), and then several of them
can meet the expired token at once.
Each would renew, but a refresh rotates the refresh token, so the second
refresh would present a token that is no longer valid.
So renewal holds a lock, and a request renews only if the token it was
refused with is still the current one; otherwise another request has
renewed already and it simply retries with the new token.

Every request also has a timeout.
Some FBF endpoints never answer---an exploration found one that hangs until
the connection is reset---and without a timeout the program would hang with
them.
The read timeout is generous, since a large list can take a while.

<<imports>>=
import threading
<<constants>>=
JSONAPI_ACCEPT = "application/vnd.api+json"
TIMEOUT = (10, 120)
<<FBF methods>>=
def request(self, method, path, **kwargs):
    """Send a request to `path` and return the `requests` response."""
    url = api_url_for(self.login.api_url, path)
    token = self.login.access_token
    response = self._send(method, url, token, **kwargs)
    if response.status_code == 401:
        with self.renewal:
            if self.login.access_token == token:
                self.renew()
        response = self._send(method, url, self.login.access_token, **kwargs)
    if not response.ok:
        raise APIError(response.status_code, method, url)
    return response

def _send(self, method, url, token, **kwargs):
    headers = {"Authorization": f"Bearer {token}", "Accept": JSONAPI_ACCEPT}
    headers.update(kwargs.pop("headers", {}))
    kwargs.setdefault("timeout", TIMEOUT)
    try:
        return self.session.request(method, url, headers=headers, **kwargs)
    except requests.exceptions.Timeout:
        raise APIError("timeout", method, url)
@

Renewal first tries the refresh token, which costs one request, and falls
back on a new launch, which costs half a dozen but needs only the Canvas
token.
The refresh rotates the refresh token too, so we keep both new tokens.

<<constants>>=
TOKEN_PATH = "/oauth/token"
TOKEN_CLIENT_ID = "FeedbackFruits"
<<FBF methods>>=
def renew(self):
    """Get new tokens: refresh them, or else launch again."""
    if self.login.refresh_token:
        response = self.session.post(
            api_url_for(self.login.api_url, TOKEN_PATH),
            data={"grant_type": "refresh_token",
                  "refresh_token": self.login.refresh_token,
                  "client_id": TOKEN_CLIENT_ID})
        if response.ok:
            tokens = response.json()
            self.login.access_token = tokens["access_token"]
            self.login.refresh_token = tokens.get(
                "refresh_token", self.login.refresh_token)
            return
    if not self.relaunch:
        raise APIError(401, "POST", api_url_for(self.login.api_url,
                                                TOKEN_PATH))
    self.login = self.relaunch()
@

\subsection{Getting documents}

[[get]] returns a [[Document]] with all pages merged, as discussed above.
The [[params]] are JSON:API query parameters such as
[[{"include": "user", "filter[id]": "a,b"}]].

<<FBF methods>>=
def get(self, path, **params):
    """Return the `Document` at `path`, all pages merged."""
    document = self.request("GET", path, params=params).json()
    if not isinstance(document.get("data"), list):
        return Document(document)
    pages = [document]
    page = next_page(document.get("meta"))
    while page:
        params["page[number]"] = page
        pages.append(self.request("GET", path, params=params).json())
        page = next_page(pages[-1].get("meta"))
    return Document(merge_pages(pages))
@

\subsection{Following relationships}

The data we want is spread over related resources: an activity has tasks, a
task has progresses, a progress has an actor.
[[related]] follows a relationship of a resource.
It prefers the [[data]] reference when the resource is already in an
[[included]] list we hold, which saves a request, and otherwise fetches the
[[related]] link.
When neither exists, the relationship is empty and we return [[None]] (or
an empty list).

There is a third case, and FBF has it: references without a link, as in an
activity's [[tasks]].
The related resources exist, but we cannot know where to fetch them from
their type alone ([[multimedia-tasks]] lives under [[/v1/tasks]]).
Returning an empty list there would be the worst answer, a silent wrong
one, so we raise [[LookupError]] and point to the remedy: fetch the resource
with [[include]] set to the relationship and pass its [[included]].

<<FBF methods>>=
def related(self, resource, name, included=None):
    """Return the resources related to `resource` through `name`.

    Returns a list for to-many relationships, a resource or `None` for
    to-one.  `included` is an index from `Document.included` to look in
    before fetching.
    """
    relationship = resource.get("relationships", {}).get(name) or {}
    data = relationship.get("data")
    references = data if isinstance(data, list) else [data] if data else []
    if included is not None and references and all(
            (r["type"], r["id"]) in included for r in references):
        found = [included[(r["type"], r["id"])] for r in references]
        return found if isinstance(data, list) else found[0]
    link = (relationship.get("links") or {}).get("related")
    if link:
        document = self.get(link)
        return document.resources if document.is_list else document.resource
    if references:
        raise LookupError(
            f"{resource['type']}.{name}: references but no link; "
            f"get the resource with include={name!r} and pass its included")
    return [] if isinstance(data, list) else None
@

The decisions of [[related]] that need no network: references found in
[[included]], an empty relationship, and references we cannot resolve.
Here a client without a session suffices, since none of these cases sends a
request.

<<test functions>>=
TASK = {"type": "instruct-tasks", "id": "t1"}
ACTIVITY = {"type": "activities", "id": "a1", "relationships": {
  "tasks": {"data": [{"type": "instruct-tasks", "id": "t1"}]},
  "note": {"data": None}, "folder": {"data": []}}}

def test_related_uses_included():
  fbf = FBF(login=None)
  included = index_resources([TASK])
  assert fbf.related(ACTIVITY, "tasks", included) == [TASK]

def test_related_empty_relationships():
  fbf = FBF(login=None)
  assert fbf.related(ACTIVITY, "note") is None
  assert fbf.related(ACTIVITY, "folder") == []

def test_related_refuses_unresolvable_references():
  import pytest
  with pytest.raises(LookupError, match="include='tasks'"):
    FBF(login=None).related(ACTIVITY, "tasks")
@


\section{Fetching in parallel}
\label{Parallel}

Some data can only be had one small request at a time: a peer-review
activity keeps its reviews per reviewer--reviewee pair, which makes some
800~requests for one activity.
At about 0.17~seconds each that is over two minutes, nearly all of it spent
waiting for FBF.
[[get_many]] sends such requests from a few threads at once.
We keep the number of threads small: FBF is a shared service, and a handful
of parallel requests is what its own web app makes when it loads a page.
The results come back in the order of the requests, so callers can zip them
with what they asked for.

<<constants>>=
WORKERS = 6
<<imports>>=
import concurrent.futures
<<FBF methods>>=
def get_many(self, requests):
    """Return the `Document`s for `requests`, fetched in parallel.

    `requests` is a list of `(path, params)` pairs; the documents come back
    in the same order.
    """
    with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(WORKERS) as pool:
        return list(pool.map(lambda request: self.get(request[0],
                                                      **request[1]),
                             requests))
@

Let's verify the order and the renewal logic without a network: a session
that answers [[401]] to the expired token stands in for FBF.
To make the requests really meet the expired token together, the fake
holds each refused request at a barrier until all the workers have arrived;
without that, the threads would take turns and the test would prove
nothing.
Likewise the refresh takes a moment, as a real one does, so that the other
threads would check the token while it is under way if the lock did not
make them wait.
It is a hand-written fake (rung~4 of the testing ladder), because what we
need---many threads meeting an expired token at once---cannot be recorded.
The refresh must happen once, however many requests failed.

<<test functions>>=
import threading as _threading
import time
from types import SimpleNamespace

class ExpiringSession:
  def __init__(self):
    self.lock = _threading.Lock()
    self.refreshes = 0
    self.together = _threading.Barrier(WORKERS, timeout=10)
  def request(self, method, url, headers=None, **kwargs):
    token = headers["Authorization"].split()[-1]
    if token == "old":
      self.together.wait()
      return SimpleNamespace(status_code=401, ok=False)
    path = url.split("example.com/")[-1]
    return SimpleNamespace(status_code=200, ok=True,
                           json=lambda: {"data": {"type": "x", "id": path}})
  def post(self, url, data=None, **kwargs):
    with self.lock:
      self.refreshes += 1
    time.sleep(0.2)
    return SimpleNamespace(ok=True, json=lambda: {
      "access_token": f"new{self.refreshes}", "refresh_token": "r2"})

def test_get_many_keeps_order_and_refreshes_once():
  session = ExpiringSession()
  login = SimpleNamespace(access_token="old", refresh_token="r1",
                          api_url="https://api.example.com")
  fbf = FBF(login, session=session)
  paths = [f"v1/x/{n}" for n in range(20)]
  documents = fbf.get_many([(path, {}) for path in paths])
  assert [d.resource["id"] for d in documents] == paths
  assert session.refreshes == 1
@


\section{Fetching by ids}

Many relationships in FBF are bare references, ids without a link, and many
lists name their related resources only by id: the authors of posts, the
choices of a quiz question, the levels of a rubric.
FBF's own web app fetches those with a list endpoint and [[filter[id]]], a
comma-separated list of ids, and so do we.
[[get_by_ids]] does it in batches, since a URL has a practical length limit,
and keeps only the resources we asked for: FBF ignores the filters an
endpoint does not support and then returns everything.

<<constants>>=
ID_BATCH = 50
<<FBF methods>>=
def get_by_ids(self, path, ids):
    """Map the `ids` to their resources, from the list endpoint `path`.

    Uses `filter[id]` in batches; ids FBF does not return are missing from
    the result.
    """
    ids = sorted(set(ids) - {None})
    batches = [ids[start:start + ID_BATCH]
               for start in range(0, len(ids), ID_BATCH)]
    documents = self.get_many([(path, {"filter[id]": ",".join(batch)})
                               for batch in batches])
    wanted = set(ids)
    return {resource["id"]: resource for document in documents
            for resource in document.resources if resource["id"] in wanted}
@

The guard against an ignored filter is what this test checks: the fake
returns an extra resource, which must not reach the result.

<<test functions>>=
class EverythingSession:
  def request(self, method, url, headers=None, params=None, **kwargs):
    asked = params["filter[id]"].split(",")
    return SimpleNamespace(status_code=200, ok=True, json=lambda: {
      "data": [{"type": "users", "id": id} for id in asked + ["stranger"]]})

def test_get_by_ids_batches_and_keeps_only_asked():
  login = SimpleNamespace(access_token="t", refresh_token=None,
                          api_url="https://api.example.com")
  fbf = FBF(login, session=EverythingSession())
  ids = [f"u{n}" for n in range(120)] + [None, "u1"]
  users = fbf.get_by_ids("v1/users", ids)
  assert sorted(users) == sorted(f"u{n}" for n in range(120))
@


\subsection{Against FBF}

The tests above stop where the network starts.
The ones below talk to FBF, through recordings in replay and for real with
[[--live]] (\cref{Testing}), so they check what holds on any day: the shape
of what comes back and how resources point to each other, not values.

[[get]] on the current user is the simplest request there is, and it returns
a single resource, not a list.
